  "notes": "Mario Botta's building (1995) on the north-east side of Third Street, facing south-west across it to Yerba Buena Gardens, now set on the South of Market grid, which runs at 45 degrees (Third Street runs north-west to south-east; read off the streets in the OpenStreetMap ground; the rectilinear parts are rotated boxes drawn as meshes): symmetrical brick tiers stepping back over a granite base with stone bands between; the black-and-white striped granite cylinder over the atrium, sliced by the skylight on a slope falling toward the street; the entrance on the axis. Sn\u00f8hetta's addition (2016, ten storeys) behind to the north-east: a tall narrow white volume, the long east face rippled (over 700 FRP panels, drawn as horizontal ripples), stepping down toward Howard Street to the south-east with the Howard Street entrance under it; the living wall on the terrace between. Yerba Buena Gardens' lawns and trees across Third Street. Refined 24 Sep 2026 without photographs, from SFMOMA's architecture page, Sn\u00f8hetta and the press (designboom, Dezeen, Metropolis) and the ground file's street lines: the first model faced due west with Third Street running true north-south; it is now turned to the real grid and placed so Third Street falls where the ground has it, the cylinder's slice is cut cell by cell along the rotated slope, the Howard Street entrance (on the addition's south-east end, with Howard Street itself) and the window toward Mission added; skylight strips on the Botta galleries' roofs either side of the cylinder. The addition's height, 204 ft (62 m), is confirmed by its facade contractor (Enclos). Guessed: tier heights and setbacks, the addition's step and footprint, its sculpted roofline (drawn flat); the St. Regis tower next door is left out. No photographs were available."
}
